=== start ===
# The NPC is at a café, scrolling through an article about the economic initiative on their phone.

#speaker: Player  
Hey, reading about the initiative? They’re selling it as a win for everyone, but doesn’t it seem like there’s more to the story?

* [If you dig deeper, you’ll see that these initiatives rarely deliver on their promises for ordinary people.] -> logical_tone
* [It’s infuriating. They make it sound like it’s for us, but it’s always the corporations that reap the rewards.] -> emotional_tone
* [Have you noticed how the wealthiest always seem to benefit most from policies like this?] -> argumentative_tone
* [I’m not saying it’s all bad, but maybe we should consider who this really benefits.] -> reserved_tone

=== logical_tone ===
#speaker: Player  
If you dig deeper, you’ll see that these initiatives rarely deliver on their promises for ordinary people. The data just doesn’t back it up.

#speaker: other  
That’s true, but what if this one is different? Maybe this time, it’ll work out.

* [I’d love for that to be true, but past patterns suggest we should remain skeptical.] -> logical_tone
* [I hope so too, but can we afford to blindly trust them again?] -> emotional_tone
* [That’s what they always say—“this time is different”—but it rarely is.] -> argumentative_tone
* [You could be right, but it’s worth being cautious.] -> reserved_tone

=== emotional_tone ===
#speaker: Player  
It’s infuriating. They make it sound like it’s for us, but it’s always the corporations that reap the rewards. When does it end?

#speaker: other  
I know, but maybe we just need to accept that the system is what it is.

* [But we can change the system if we stop accepting things that hurt us.] -> logical_tone
* [That’s a defeatist way to look at it. We deserve better, don’t you think?] -> emotional_tone
* [That’s exactly what they want us to believe—that we’re powerless to stop them.] -> argumentative_tone
* [Maybe so. But even small steps can lead to meaningful change.] -> reserved_tone

=== argumentative_tone ===
#speaker: Player  
Have you noticed how the wealthiest always seem to benefit most from policies like this? It’s no coincidence.

#speaker: other  
You might be right, but don’t those benefits eventually help everyone?

* [Trickle-down economics has been debunked. The wealth rarely trickles down—it stays at the top.] -> logical_tone
* [But how long should we wait for those benefits to reach the rest of us?] -> emotional_tone
* [That’s the lie they sell to keep us complacent. We need to demand better.] -> argumentative_tone
* [Maybe, but let’s make sure we fully understand what’s at stake first.] -> reserved_tone

=== reserved_tone ===
#speaker: Player  
I’m not saying it’s all bad, but maybe we should consider who this really benefits. Sometimes it’s worth taking a step back.

#speaker: other  
That’s fair. It’s always good to take a moment to evaluate the full picture.

* [Exactly. If it’s truly for everyone, the benefits will be clear without the need for spin.] -> logical_tone
* [I just don’t want us to fall into the same trap as before. We’ve seen this play out too often.] -> emotional_tone
* [We owe it to ourselves to question their motives. Change starts with awareness.] -> argumentative_tone
* [Take your time. I’m here if you need someone to bounce ideas off of.] -> reserved_tone